(module crux-shape-accepts-malformed)
;; THE PER-ROW CHECK DROPPED. rfc_to_implementation.py's check_extraction runs
;; five clauses over EVERY normative row (:403 the row is an object, :405 it
;; carries all seven required keys, :407 its line bounds are integers, :410 they
;; are in order, :422 its rule matches N followed by digits). This body keeps
;; the whole-document clauses and drops the per-row conjunct, so an extraction
;; whose rows are arbitrary passes as long as the envelope is right.
;;
;; WHY THIS IS THE FAILURE THAT MATTERS AND NOT A COSMETIC ONE. The rows are
;; what reconcile.py consumes, and it matches by LINE SPAN: :407's own message
;; says "reconciliation matches by line span, so these must be numbers". A
;; census that passes this validator with a string line_start reaches stage E,
;; which shells out to a tool that will do arithmetic on it. The clause is a
;; guard on the next stage's input, not a tidiness check on this one's output,
;; which is why dropping it is worth freezing a verdict against.
;;
;; DISCRIMINATION, measured at v0.14.87 over this body:
;;
;;   [D7-OBJECT] + [D7-LISTS] + [D7-NONEMPTY] + [D7-NO-HARDCODE]  -> SAFE
;;   [D7-ROWS] alone                                              -> REFUTED
;;
;; [D7-NO-HARDCODE] survives, and that is the interesting half. This mutant is
;; strictly WEAKER than the honest decision, so the converse implication still
;; holds: every conforming extraction does pass. An anti-hardcoding post cannot
;; catch a validator that accepts too much, and a forward post cannot catch one
;; that accepts too little. The pair is what covers both directions, and this
;; crux and crux-shape-row-count-fitted.llmll are the two halves that make that
;; observable in the gate rather than only argued in a comment.
(def extraction-conforms? [is-object: bool
                           has-normative: bool
                           has-excluded: bool
                           normative-count: int
                           rows-conforming: int] -> bool

  (pre (and (>= normative-count 0)
            (and (>= rows-conforming 0)
                 (<= rows-conforming normative-count))))

  (post (=> result is-object))
  (post (=> result (and has-normative has-excluded)))
  (post (=> result (> normative-count 0)))
  (post (=> result (= rows-conforming normative-count)))

  (post (=> (and is-object
                 (and has-normative
                      (and has-excluded
                           (and (> normative-count 0)
                                (= rows-conforming normative-count))))) result))

  (and is-object
       (and has-normative
            (and has-excluded
                 (> normative-count 0)))))
